            <ArticleTitle>The optimal time of elective percutaneous coronary intervention for stable patients after ST elevation myocardial infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>67-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0003319708314248</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To find the optimal time (early: &lt; or =3 days; late: &gt;3 days) for revascularization in ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) patients in the subacute phase.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ninety-nine STEMI patients who were admitted to Gazi University Faculty of Medicine between 2000 and 2004 were enrolled into this study. Patients were divided into 2 groups according to time from the beginning of symptoms to the percutaneous coronary intervention. Coronary angiograms before and after the revascularization were evaluated using the quantitative coronary angiogram technique.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">45 early (group I) and 54 late (group II) revascularized patients were evaluated. There were no significant differences between the 2 groups regarding demographic properties, thrombus score, success of the procedure, quantitative angiographic parameters, and clinical results of the procedure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Waiting for the development of stable phase in STEMI to apply PCI has no obvious benefit for angiographic and clinical results.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
